00:29:50.810 --> 00:29:55.810
Samuel Smith: Who the actual signing requester was, because you're using a bearer token.

224
00:29:55.890 --> 00:30:12.560
Samuel Smith: So… so that means that anytime you are doing secure signing, you have to look at this whole thing and decide what the side channel attacks are for this for you to say that something is secure. So when the NIST says there's a crypto period.

225
00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:18.580
Samuel Smith: or a private key, they're referring to all of the ways that… that the…

226
00:30:18.580 --> 00:30:37.190
Samuel Smith: exposure could result in fraudulent signatures. Not just narrow things like, did you do a cache timing attack, or an out-of-order execution attack on the signing hardware, but did you do an attack on your access control API, or the way that you authenticate signing requesters?

227
00:30:38.490 --> 00:30:50.430
Samuel Smith: Which means that other keys become… other key compromises or other authentication factors, like passwords, become side-channel attack vectors for your signing infrastructure.

